OpenVDB 13.0 (7c03e1f0): exact fast path for VolumeToMesh's adaptive merge
and leaner adaptive point and polygon passes. Output is byte-identical to the
unpatched header on every input tested (below). Applies after
0001-flat-quad-output.patch.

Applied to vendor/PicoGKRuntime/openvdb by scripts/fetch-deps.sh. After
changing this patch, re-run scripts/build-deps-wasm.sh (both variants) so the
dependency prefixes reinstall the header.

tools/VolumeToMesh.h, adaptive path only (adaptivity > 0; a = 0 is untouched):
- MergeVoxelRegions: for float grids without a spatial adaptivity grid, each
  leaf runs exactFastMergeLeaf. It works on bit rows: the sign and point-index
  activity masks become rows along z, so the ambiguity, missing-point and dim-2
  non-manifold tests cover many voxels per integer operation. The leaf and the
  neighbour leaves it reads are copied into one dense 10^3 brick, so the CD_2ND
  gradients need no accessor lookups. Normals are stored as structure-of-arrays
  floats in fixed scratch arrays (no allocation per leaf).
- The merge predicate (1 - float dot) > a in double is monotone in the float
  dot, so it is replaced by dot < T, where T is the smallest float that passes
  (found with nextafter). Each parent block tests only the pairs that cross its
  children, because pairs within a child already passed. A cone test (mean
  direction, 2e-6 and 1e-5 margins over the float error bounds) accepts big
  uniform blocks without pairs. It only ever accepts blocks that every pair
  would accept. Region ids are given in stock order.
- The dim-2 isNonManifold result of the pre-pass is reused instead of being
  evaluated a second time in the merge loop.
- Leaves whose gradients are not finite fall back to the stock loop, and so do
  grids that are not float or that carry a spatial adaptivity grid.
- ComputePoints: a stable counting sort for region ids < 128 replaces the
  std::map of vectors; ids >= 128 keep the map. AdaptiveLeafNodePointCount
  uses a bitset for ids < 128. AdaptivePrimBuilder reuses its scratch vectors
  per range and allocates each pool once at its exact size.

Exactness assumes the compiler does not contract a*b+c into FMA differently
from the stock build. wasm has no FMA, and x86-64-v2 has none; build with
-ffp-contract=off where FMA is available. Evidence: byte equality on every
configuration (a = 0 and 0.001..1 x relax off/on, 1 and 4 threads) of
box/geode/lamp/lattice at 0.5 and 0.25 mm and geode at 0.15 mm, ~66 k fuzz
comparisons (thin walls, creases, saddles, quantized and noise fields, tiles,
fog and level sets, adaptivity and surface masks, thresholds at an observed
pair dot +-1 ulp), and wasm serial and pthreads digests equal to stock on
box/geode/lamp/lampxl. Mutation tests: 7 seeded bugs, every one caught.
